course: dictionary
lesson: 12876
title: Ensnare: caught by a trap or a trick
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To ensnare something is to catch it in a snare, which is a trap. Animals can be ensnared, and so can people. «The spider waited to ensnare the fly in its sticky web.» The web catches the fly, so ensnare means to catch in a trap.
explain: The second meaning is about people. «The scammer tried to ensnare Mia with a fake prize email.» Mia could be tricked into a bad situation that is hard to get out of. Look at the clues around the word: trick, fake and hard to get out of all point to the same meaning.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. "The spider waited to catch the fly in its sticky web" still makes sense, so you are on the right track.
fact: Ensnare means to catch something in a snare or trap, or to trick someone into a bad situation they cannot easily escape.
fact: «The hunter set a wire loop to ensnare a rabbit.» Here the rabbit is caught in a trap.
fact: People can be ensnared by a trick, such as a fake prize, and then find it hard to get free.
fact: Common phrases are «ensnared in» and «ensnared by», as in «ensnared in a lie» or «ensnared by a clever plan».
fact: The forms of the word are ensnare, ensnares, ensnared and ensnaring: «The web ensnares the fly» and «The fly was ensnared».
exercise: «The spider waited to ensnare the fly.» What does ensnare mean here? | To wash it clean | To let it go free | *To catch in a trap | To feed it a meal
exercise: «The scammer tried to ensnare Mia with a fake prize.» What does ensnare mean here? | To take her to the shops | To make her laugh | To give her a gift | *To trick her into a bad situation
exercise: Ben was ___ in a web of lies. Which word fits the gap? | ensnares | *ensnared | ensnaring | ensnare
test: Yesterday the hunter ___ two rabbits in his trap. Which word fits the gap? | ensnaring | ensnare | *ensnared | ensnares
test: Today the trap ___ every mouse that comes near. Which word fits the gap? | ensnared | ensnare | ensnaring | *ensnares
exam: «Sam was ensnared by the game's secret rules.» What does ensnared mean here? | Given a prize for winning | Freed from danger | Bored and sleepy | *Caught in a bad situation that is hard to escape
